ABOUT = """<h2>About this map</h2>
<p>Every decade or so someone reads the law of the sea, or the Outer Space Treaty, and notices that certain places belong to no country. They then try to found one there, with a flag, a constitution, coins and sometimes a tower or a raft. This map collects those attempts, on the sea, the seabed, in the air, in orbit, on Antarctic ice, on the Moon and in the small strips of land two neighbours each decline to claim. It also includes the nearest comparisons on land: micronations, zones granted by a host government, and governments that run a territory nobody recognises.</p>
<p>The first thing to know is that being outside a country does not mean being outside its law. Ships answer to the flag they fly, and a ship with no flag can be boarded. Spacecraft stay under the state that registered them, along with the people aboard. Your citizenship, your bank, your suppliers and the radio licence you need all keep a line open to some government. Most of the failures here happened along one of those lines, and not at the place itself.</p>
<p>The second thing is that there are several different ambitions. Living beyond land is already possible. Running your own rules among people who agree to them is often possible. Using your own money among yourselves is easy. Keeping states away for ten years is hard. Being recognised as a state by others has not happened to any new founding on the open sea, on ice or in space. So every place on this map carries separate odds for each of those.</p>
<h3>How to read it</h3>
<p>The shaded zones and the list called "Every place, top to bottom" give the estimated odds for each kind of location, and the percentages change with the shading menu. Click a place for its rules, the state's way of reaching you there, the figures and the attempts made there. Each mark is an attempt: its shape shows where it sits, and its colour how it turned out. Some marks are laws or court tests, listed so you can see where a rule came from. Places in orbit and beyond cannot be pinned to the surface, so those attempts sit at the founder's base or launch site on Earth, and the Moon and Mars buttons show the landers and rovers that are physically there.</p>
<p>The Patterns box tells the story across cases. The Command room gathers the comparisons: the full table of odds, how far attempts got, who stopped them, what things cost, and a list of everything a founder would have to deal with today, from statehood tests and UN votes to bank access, passports, launch licences, radio spectrum and the risk of breaking securities law when selling citizenship.</p>
<h3>What the map does not do</h3>
<p>It gives no legal advice, and it does not recommend any attempt. The odds are our estimates of what could happen, reasoned from the record. Real attempts have ended in arrest, in fraud cases and in deaths at sea.</p>"""
METHOD = """<h2>Method and sources</h2>
<h3>What is counted</h3>
<p>__NA__ attempts to found or hold self-governing territory, and __NL__ entries for laws, treaties, court tests and hardware that show where the rules come from. An entry counts as an attempt when someone tried to set up a place, a people or a government outside the usual authority of a state, or set up a private community or carve-out that serves as the nearest comparison. Nothing was left out for being small or silly, because hobby claims show how states respond when nothing is at stake. Records draw on __NS__ web pages retrieved while building the map, and each box lists its own. A separate checking pass then compared the records with their sources and made about 180 corrections, many of them later outcomes. That pass was uneven: many records were checked against only one or two of their sources, and 14 entries on air, orbit and online claims were not checked at all, including Cloud Nine, Wirtland and the Chicago Convention entry.</p>
<h3>The odds are estimates</h3>
<p>No study measures these chances, so they are editorial judgments for this map, and they would be different in another author's hands. Each assumes a serious private group with real money and no state backing. They are rough bands, so 3% and 5% should be read as the same size of thing. The reasoning for each is written out in the box for that place.</p>
<ul><li><b>Physically get there and stay:</b> people living there within 25 years.</li><li><b>Run your own rules, use your own currency, hold off outside states:</b> assuming people are already there. Holding means ten years with no state boarding, arresting, closing or absorbing it. A ship with a flag holds because it accepts that flag state.</li><li><b>Overall</b> is getting there multiplied by holding it.</li><li><b>Recognition:</b> at least one UN member formally recognises it as a state within 25 years.</li></ul>
<h3>How far each attempt got</h3>
<p>The steps in each attempt's box come from what the record shows: built, inhabited, a currency, laws or courts, outlasting a state's challenge, recognition by a UN member. "Outlasted" and "recognised" were assigned by hand, and are generous to the attempt where the record is unclear. How each one ended is sorted into five groups, and "stopped or absorbed by a state" includes peaceful divisions by treaty.</p>
<h3>Limits of the record</h3>
<ul><li>Many small claims are documented only by Wikipedia or their own websites. Where nothing better could be retrieved, this is the source listed, and the box says so through its source list.</li><li>Positions marked approximate are estimates. Attempts in orbit or beyond are placed at a founder's base or launch site. A few proposals, such as Freedom Ship, had no site at all, and their marks are placeholders the text describes.</li><li>Some candidates could not be verified and are left out, including Republic of Lomar, Tierra Libre and the Aquatica habitat.</li><li>Later events may have overtaken any entry. Research ended in November 2026.</li></ul>
<h3>Map data</h3>
<p>High seas and the Clarion-Clipperton Zone: Marine Regions, Flanders Marine Institute, CC BY. The zone between coast and high seas is what remains after removing high seas and land, so it combines exclusive economic zones with territorial seas. Land and disputed areas: Natural Earth. Gornja Siga: OpenStreetMap. Antarctic sectors drawn from the claimants' stated longitudes, and drawn from 60°S, the Antarctic Treaty's limit. Moon: NASA Scientific Visualization Studio CGI Moon Kit. Mars: NASA Mars Trek, Viking MDIM 2.1 colour mosaic. Basemaps are shared with the other Welcome to Your Galaxy maps.</p>"""
